WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.979
It’s hard to figure out just what elevates
an actor’s performance into greatness.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.260
Sometimes it’s commitment.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.440
Other times it’s technical skill, the sheer
ability of a performer to portray the subtleties

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.420
and nuances of a challenging character.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.700
Often the right role simply fits an actor
like a glove.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.970
And occasionally, if everything plays out
just right, all these things can happen at

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.080
once, making for some of the greatest moments
in movie history.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.880
These are a few of those moments.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.060
Becoming less than human

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.710
There's a tremendous degree of difficulty
in performing the role of a human stripped

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.509
of their humanity, someone whose soul has
been ravaged to the point of no return.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:36.960
And no actor has managed it quite like Choi
Min-sik in Park Chan-wook’s brutal masterpiece

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.800
Oldboy.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.430
Choi stars as Oh Dae-su, a man out for revenge
after being imprisoned in a hotel room under

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.020
mysterious circumstances by unknown forces.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.040
There’s little recognizable humanity in
Dae-su when he first emerges from his makeshift

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.530
prison, and what remains has been stripped
away by the film's climax.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.879
Choi scrapes down into the absolute depths
of the human soul and offers a turn which

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.360
audiences will likely never forget, no matter
how much they’d like to.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.020
Digging deep

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.420
Gavin O'Connor's 2011 drama Warrior is one
of the unsung triumphs of the decade.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:20.250
What could have been a glorified Lifetime
cable drama instead turned out as a heart-wrenching

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.369
story about fathers, sons, and brothers.

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:26.689
Joel Edgerton and Nick Nolte delivered their
own remarkable performances, but it was Tom

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:28.640
Hardy who really made the movie what it is.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.940
Hardy's character Tommy is a raw, exposed
nerve of a human being, ravaged by trauma,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.880
guilt, and hate.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.780
Hardy masks the character's confusion, sadness,
and fear with a shield of brashness that deflects

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.740
every friendly hand that comes his way.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.100
“Are we done, Brendan?

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.060
Are we finished?”

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.680
It's a testament to Hardy's own talents that
his character never becomes outright unlikable,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.320
despite the script giving us every reason
to hate him.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.320
The sum of their parts

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.119
Even the most stellar ensemble casts are outshone
by one or two standout characters.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.289
Pulp Fiction has Vincent and Jules.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:03.740
The Usual Suspects is very much Verbal's story.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.440
But the best ensemble movies are defined by
a cast of characters who all excel together.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.900
It’s rare, but it happens, and The Breakfast
Club is the best example of this kind of movie.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.030
It's impossible to pinpoint a single performance
among the main cast as the best, not only

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.390
because they're all just so good, but because
removing one piece of the ensemble would bring

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.780
everything tumbling down.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:23.580
“Now it’s eight.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:24.500
You stay out of it.”

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:25.780
“Excuse me, sir, it’s seven.”

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.800
“Shut up, Pee Wee.”

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.920
Some of the finest teen stars of the 1980s
were brought together to form the eponymous

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.460
Breakfast Club, including Molly Ringwald,
Anthony Michael Hall, Emilio Estevez, Judd

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.750
Nelson, and Ally Sheedy.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.960
Each cast member works in perfect tandem with
the other four, and every character receives

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:46.900
equal focus.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.740
There's no lead or standout actor, and the
result is a collective performance that’s

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.180
nothing less than magical.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.300
Nearing the breaking point

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.560
In Do the Right Thing, Spike Lee's Mookie
is a man adrift.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.920
Caught in his day-to-day life between working
for a family of racist Italian-Americans and

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.330
his friendships with other aggrieved African-Americans,
Mookie is a character driven by a powerful

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.740
and almost unresolvable frustration.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.230
Lee takes the character on a journey from
selfish passivity to insatiable fury, channeling

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.800
the character's anger and slowly building
towards an inevitable breaking point.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.360
“Hey!”

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.780
His performance was one of the best of the
decade, and is as powerful today as when the

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.090
movie was first released.

